{
  "_": [
    "The curated seed: shopping CONCEPTS, named in all six languages FamCart speaks.",
    "",
    "This file is the catalog's floor. It is what makes the app useful on the day a",
    "household installs it, before anyone has searched for anything and before Open",
    "Food Facts has been asked a single question. Every later row arrives because a",
    "real person went looking for something that was not here.",
    "",
    "WHY GENERICS AND NOT BRANDS. 'Milk' is what somebody writes on a shopping list.",
    "A specific 1L carton of a specific dairy's semi-skimmed milk is what they pick",
    "up in the shop, and the list does not care which. Generic concepts are first",
    "class here (spec S13): no barcode, no brand, no image, and none of that makes",
    "them low quality -- a banana genuinely has no manufacturer.",
    "",
    "EVERY NAME IS A TRANSLATION, NOT A GUESS. en is the canonical name; the other",
    "five become alias rows of type 'name', and search_catalog returns the one",
    "matching the phone's language. Nothing here is a commercial fact -- no sizes,",
    "no barcodes, no ingredients -- so there is nothing in this file that could be",
    "invented (S4). If a concept cannot be named honestly in a language, the entry",
    "leaves that language out rather than approximating it.",
    "",
    "  id   stable slug, the provenance key. NEVER reuse or repurpose one: it is",
    "       what makes a re-import update a row rather than create a second.",
    "  cat  category, from the closed list in 002_products.sql. Its own six names",
    "       live in categories.json and are attached to every product in it, so",
    "       'lactate' reaches every dairy product and not only the ones named that.",
    "  w    editorial weight. 100 = a weekly staple, 40 = occasional. This is the",
    "       ONLY ranking input the seed controls; everything above it is earned by",
    "       real people adding the product to real lists.",
    "",
    "WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: concepts you cannot shop from.",
    "",
    "You can buy a brandless toothbrush. You can buy brandless potatoes and",
    "brandless carrots, and nobody minds. You cannot usefully buy brandless",
    "SHAMPOO -- the word on its own is not a shopping instruction, it is a",
    "question, and whoever is holding the list has to guess. Same for deodorant,",
    "laundry detergent, cat food, coffee, beer, and above all baby formula, which",
    "cannot be substituted at all.",
    "",
    "So fifty such concepts were REMOVED rather than demoted: shampoo,",
    "conditioner, shower gel, deodorant, toothpaste, mouthwash, razor, shaving",
    "foam, the three creams, sunscreen, hair gel, sanitary pads, tampons; laundry",
    "detergent, fabric softener, dish soap, dishwasher tablets, bleach, the four",
    "cleaners, air freshener; nappies, baby food, baby formula, baby shampoo; dog",
    "food, cat food, cat litter, pet treats; painkillers, vitamins, cough syrup;",
    "coffee, instant coffee, tea, cola, energy drink, chocolate spread, cereal;",
    "and all the alcohol.",
    "",
    "THE TEST IS NOT 'does this category have brands' -- nearly every category",
    "does. It is: IS THE BARE WORD ENOUGH TO SHOP FROM? 'Carrots' is. 'Milk' is.",
    "'Deodorant' is not.",
    "",
    "WHAT REMOVING THEM COSTS, so it is a known price rather than a surprise. A",
    "search for 'shampoo' now finds whatever real branded products the catalog",
    "holds -- which in Romanian is Head & Shoulders and Johnson's, and in the",
    "other five languages is currently NOTHING, until discovery has run for that",
    "word. That is the intended shape: an empty result is a question discovery",
    "goes and answers, where a bare concept was an answer that satisfied nobody",
    "and stopped anyone looking.",
    "",
    "The marginal cases were deliberately KEPT, because the bare word is a",
    "perfectly good list entry: soap, toothbrush, wet wipes, plasters, toilet",
    "paper, tissues, bin bags, chocolate, biscuits, crisps, ketchup, mayonnaise,",
    "batteries, light bulbs, milk, bread, eggs."
  ],

